(*
expect:
quiet 1
flood 63
last 63
overruns 7
total 7
done
*)
(* tuvm#20 — THE GATE: the ring never kills the node. Before this fix,
   space_post_id hard-abort()ed the whole VM on the 65th undrained post
   ("channel queue full") — a burst of events bypassed the crash boundary and
   supervisor entirely, inverting let-it-crash into node death. G2 pins the
   policy instead: a full ring DROPS the newest stream post and COUNTS it
   (never unbounded, never silent).

   Trace (OEVM_SPACE_QUEUE_DEPTH = 64): one event to `quiet` first (slot 1 of
   64), then 70 posts to `flood` — 63 fit (slots 2..64), 7 overflow and are
   dropped+counted. Reaching drain AT ALL is the headline assertion (the old
   code dies at post #65). After drain: quiet's handler fired (a flooding
   channel does not starve an already-queued neighbor), flood's handler saw
   exactly the FIRST 63 events in FIFO order (drop-newest keeps history up to
   the overload point — David's call, 2026-07-14: the crash that STARTED a
   storm must not be the record lost), and the counter surfaces the 7 drops
   per channel and in the node total. *)
class StreamFlood packageUsing: manifest = ( | quietHits ::= 0. floodHits ::= 0. last ::= 0. | ) (
  public main: platform args: args = (
    | space flood quiet |
    space:: platform space.
    flood:: space channel: 'flood'.
    quiet:: space channel: 'quiet'.
    quiet whenever: '' do: [ :m | quietHits:: quietHits + 1 ].
    flood whenever: '' do: [ :m | floodHits:: floodHits + 1. last:: m ].
    quiet out: '' value: 1.
    1 to: 70 do: [ :i | flood out: '' value: i ].
    space drain.
    ('quiet ' , quietHits printString) out.
    ('flood ' , floodHits printString) out.
    ('last ' , last printString) out.
    ('overruns ' , (platform spaceOverruns: 'flood') printString) out.
    ('total ' , (platform spaceOverruns: '') printString) out.
    'done' out.
  )
) : (
)
